<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.9.3: Vertex Form Given a Vertex and Point</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02473</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.9.3: Vertex Form Given a Vertex and Point</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e4bb58cd-78a3-4cb6-b364-a022289651a7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>In previous activities and lessons, students have worked on manipulating the different quadratic forms to find equivalent equations. In this activity, students will write the equation of a quadratic function in vertex form given a point and a vertex. Ask the class for the vertex form of a quadratic and display it on the board for reference throughout the activity.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Switch pairs from activity 9.9.2 and repeat the sage and scribe strategy.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking, Writing<title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking, Writing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>For the Sage or Scribe activity, allow the ELL student to choose the role where they are most comfortable, first.</para>
    <para>When speaking as the sage, students should focus on practicing to</para>
    <list>
      <item>distinguish sounds and intonation patterns of English with increasing ease</item>
      <item>practice producing sounds of newly acquired vocabulary such as long and short vowels, silent letters, and consonant clusters to pronounce English words in a manner that is increasingly comprehensible</item>
      <item>learn relationships between sounds and letters of the English language and decode (sound out) words using a combination of skills such as recognizing sound-letter relationships and identifying cognates, affixes, roots, and base words</item>
    </list>
    <para>When writing the steps as the scribe and describing the steps on paper during their feedback reflection students will work to:</para>
    <list>
      <item>learn relationships between sounds and letters of the English language to represent sounds when writing in English</item>
      <item>recognize elements of the English sound system in newly acquired vocabulary such as long and short vowels, silent letters, and consonant clusters</item>
    </list>
    <para>Monitor English language learners for progress on the above as they listen, write, and speak.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Optimize output</para>
</div>
</div>
<title>Student Activity </title>
<para>For questions 1 - 2, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at (5, 8) and also goes through the point \((-3, 6)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Write the equation in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=\frac{-1}{32}(x-5)^2+8\)</para>
<para>For questions 3 and 4, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-4, 3)\) and also goes through the point \((2, 5)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Write the equation in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=\frac{1}{18}(x+4)^2+3\)</para>
<para>For questions 5 - 6, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((6, -2)\) and also goes through the point \((3, 1)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> Write the equation in vertex form. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=\frac{1}{3}(x-6)^2-2\)</para>
<title>Video: Writing an Quadratic Equation from a Vertex and a Point</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing a quadratic equation from a vertex and a point.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><iframe allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen="" frameborder="0" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/1IUEVwIjywc?si=lbcWBG5YBlTAOW1k;&amp;rel=0" title="YouTube video player"/></iframe>
</div>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Students may not substitute the points in the correct places in the formula. It can be helpful for students to write the vertex in one color and point in another and also color code the vertex formula to match so \(h\) and \(k\) are the color of the vertex point and \(x\) and \(y\) are the color of the additional point.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>If time allows, have students create their own problems where they choose a vertex and a point and have their partners write the equation in vertex form. Partners can also try to “stump” other partners. As an extension, have students use graphing technology to graph the point and vertex of each quadratic and graph the quadratic to see how the graph connects to the equation and the points also lie on the parabola. This can also provide time to quickly discuss any key characteristics in the graphs that were discussed in unit 7 and earlier in unit 9.</para>
<title>9.9.3: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following is the quadratic equation of the quadratic with a vertex of \((4, -2)\) that also goes through the point \((6, 5)\)?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=\frac47(x-4)^2-2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: When solving for \(a\), be sure to divide correctly. The correct answer is \(y=\frac74(x-4)^2-2\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=\frac74(x-4)^2-2\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Substitute the vertex and point into the vertex form to solve for \(a\), \(5=a(6-4)^2-2 \) so \(a=\frac74\). Then write the equation, \(y=\frac74(x-4)^2-2\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=\frac74(x-6)^2-5\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Substitute the vertex \((4, -2)\) in for \((h, k)\), not the point. The correct answer is \(y=\frac74(x-4)^2-2\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=\frac47(x-6)^2-5\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Substitute the vertex \((4, -2)\) in for \((h, k)\), not the point and be sure to divide correctly when finding the slope. The correct answer is \(y=\frac74(x-4)^2-2\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>9.9.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
<para>Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((3, 4)\) and goes through the point \((1, 2)\).</para>
<para>First start with the vertex form of a quadratic function.</para>
<para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Substitute the vertex in at \((h, k)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis>- Solve for \(a\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) value and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form.</para>
<title>Try It: Creating Vertex Form from a Vertex and a Point</title>
<para>Write the quadratic in vertex form that has a vertex at \((2, 5)\) and goes through the point \((3, 7)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=2(x-2)^2+5\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Substitute the vertex in at \((h, k)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the other point at \((x, y)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for \(a\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Substitute the value of \(a\) value and the vertex \((h, k)\) into the vertex form.</para>
<title>Video: Writing an Quadratic Equation from a Vertex and a Point</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing a quadratic equation from a vertex and a point.</para>
